{"text": "5891\n\nRemoval of MAP4 from microtubules in\nvivo produces no observable phenotype at\n\nthe cellular level\n\nMicrotubule associated protein 4 (MAP4)\nMT assembly in vitro and is localized along\nSeren vivo, These resultsand the fact that MAPA\njhe major MAP in nonneuronal cells suggest that\n'MAP4\u2019s normal functions may include the stabilization\n\u2018of MTs in situ. To understend MAPA function in vivo,\nwwe produced a blocking antibody (Ab) to prevent\n\u2018MAPS binding to MTs. The COOH-terminal MT bind-\n{ag domain of MAPA was expressed in Escherichia coli\nasa glutathione transferase fusion protein and was\nJected into rabbits to produce an antiserum that was\nthen affinity purified and shown to be monospecific for\nMAPA. This Ab blocked >95% of MAPS binding to\nMTs in an in vitro assay. Microinjection ofthe affinity\nied Ab into human fibroblasts and monkey epithe-\ncells abolished MAPS binding to MTs as assayed\nrat polyclonal antibody agains the NH.-terminal\nprojection domain of MAP4. The removal of MAPS\n\n{rom MTs was accompanied by its sequestration into\nvisible MAPS-Ab immunocomplenes. However. the\nMT network appeared normal. Tubulin photoactiv\ntion and nocodazole sensitivity assays indicated that\nMT dynamics were not altered detectably by the re\nmoval of MAPA from the MTS, Cells progressed to mi-\ntosis with mosphologically normal spindles in the ab-\nsence of MAPA binding to MTs. Depleting MAP4 from\nMTs also did not affect the state of posttranslational\n\u2018modifications of tubulin subunits. Further, no perturbs-\ntions of MT-dependent organelle distribution were de-\ntected. We conclude thatthe association of MAPA with\nMTs is not essential for MT assembly or for the MT-\nbased functions in cultured cells that we could assay. A\nSignificant role for MAP4 isnot excluded by these re-\nsults. however. as MAP4 may be a component of a\nfunctionally redundant system.\n\n\u2018Wang, M., Peloquin, J.G., Zhai, Y., Bulinski, J.C., and Borisy, G.G.\n\n\u2018The Journal of Cell Biology 132(3):345-357, February 1996,\n\nOther support: National Institutes of Health,\n\nFrom the Laboratory of Molecular Biology, University of Wisconsin-Madison,\nMadison, WI, and Department of Anatomy and Cell Biology, Columbia University\nCollege of Physicians and Surgeons, New York, NY.\n\nPUBLICATIONS\n\n0052974",
